  </prolog>
   <taskbody>
      <context>
         <p>You can check the print head nozzles using a utility on your Windows or Mac computer.</p>
      </context>
      <steps id="steps_05013267">
         <step>
            <cmd>Make sure there are no errors on the LCD screen and the output tray is extended.</cmd>
         </step>
         <step>
            <cmd>Load a few sheets of plain paper in the product.</cmd>
         </step>
         <step>
            <cmd>Do one of the following:</cmd>
            <info>
               <ul id="ul_05013265">
                  <li>
                     <b>Windows:</b>Access the Windows Desktop and right-click the <image height="19" href="../media/image147.png" placement="break" width="19">
                        <alt>placeholder</alt>
                     </image> icon for your product in the right side of the Windows taskbar, or click the up arrow and right-click <image height="19" href="../media/image147.png" placement="break" width="19">
                        <alt>placeholder</alt>
                     </image>.</li>
                  <li>
                     <b>Mac</b>: In the Apple menu or the Dock, select <b>System</b>
                     <b>Preferences</b>. Select <b>Print</b>
                     <b>&amp;</b>
                     <b>Fax</b>, <b>Print</b>
                     <b>&amp; Scan</b>, or <b>Printers</b>
                     <b>&amp; Scanners</b>, select your product, and select <b>Options</b>
                     <b>&amp; Supplies</b>. Select <b>Utility</b>and select <b>Open Printer Utility</b>.</li>
               </ul>
            </info>
         </step>
         <step>
            <cmd>Select <b>Print</b>
               <b>Head</b>
               <b>Nozzle</b>
               <b>Check</b>. You see a window like this:</cmd>
            <info>
               <fig>
                  <title>placeholder</title>
                  <image height="289" href="../media/image161.jpeg" placement="break" width="300">
                     <alt>placeholder</alt>
                  </image>
               </fig>
            </info>
         </step>
         <step>
            <cmd>Click <b>Print</b>.</cmd>
         </step>
         <step>
            <cmd>Check the printed pattern to see if there are gaps in the lines.</cmd>
